Source paper: https://www.whatdotheyknow.com/request/oxford_physics_past_papers_2/response/2340696/attach/6/Physics%20Papers%202017.pdf?cookie_passthrough=1. The cover identifies Trinity Term 2018; the annual bundle's 2017 filename does not determine the examination year. We use <natural units> $\hbar=c=1$ for the <classical field theory> and <quantum field theory> in four spacetime dimensions and <Minkowski metric> $\eta=\operatorname{diag}(1,-1,-1,-1)$.

== 1
{scope}

=== a
{scope}

==== Solution

The <Euler-Lagrange equation> for the <real scalar field> gives
$$
\partial_\mu\frac{\partial\mathcal L}{\partial(\partial_\mu\phi)}-\frac{\partial\mathcal L}{\partial\phi}=0
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
\boxed{(\Box+m^2)\phi=0.}
$$
Thus the <conjugate momentum> and <Hamiltonian density> are
$$
\boxed{\pi=\frac{\partial\mathcal L}{\partial\dot\phi}=\dot\phi,\qquad
\mathcal H=\pi\dot\phi-\mathcal L=\frac12\bigl[\pi^2+(\nabla\phi)^2+m^2\phi^2\bigr],\qquad H=\int d^3x\,\mathcal H.}
$$
The spatial <Fourier transform> reduces the <Klein-Gordon equation> to $\ddot\phi_{\mathbf p}+\omega_{\mathbf p}^2\phi_{\mathbf p}=0$, with positive <frequency> $\omega_{\mathbf p}=\sqrt{\mathbf p^2+m^2}$. Hence a general real solution is the <mode expansion of a free field>
$$
\boxed{\phi(t,\mathbf x)=\int\frac{d^3p}{(2\pi)^3\sqrt{2\omega_{\mathbf p}}}
\left[a_{\mathbf p}e^{-i\omega_{\mathbf p}t+i\mathbf p\cdot\mathbf x}
+a_{\mathbf p}^*e^{i\omega_{\mathbf p}t-i\mathbf p\cdot\mathbf x}\right].}
$$
The two coefficients are complex conjugates because the <field> is real. Each pair of initial data $\phi(0,\mathbf x),\pi(0,\mathbf x)$ fixes these coefficients. The normalization anticipates <canonical quantization> and the relativistic normalization of one-particle states.

=== b
{scope}

==== Solution

Promote the <mode expansion of a free field> coefficients to <annihilation operators> and <creation operators>, replacing $a^*$ by $a^\dagger$. The <canonical commutation relations> are
$$
\boxed{[a_{\mathbf p},a_{\mathbf q}^\dagger]=(2\pi)^3\delta^3(\mathbf p-\mathbf q),\qquad
[a_{\mathbf p},a_{\mathbf q}]=[a_{\mathbf p}^\dagger,a_{\mathbf q}^\dagger]=0.}
$$
Together with the normalization in part a, they give $[\phi(t,\mathbf x),\pi(t,\mathbf y)]=i\delta^3(\mathbf x-\mathbf y)$ and vanishing equal-time <commutators> between two <fields> or two <conjugate momenta>.

Insert the <mode expansion of a free field> in the <Hamiltonian>. The spatial integral produces $(2\pi)^3\delta^3(\mathbf p\pm\mathbf q)$. The $aa$ and $a^\dagger a^\dagger$ coefficients cancel because $\omega_{\mathbf p}^2=\mathbf p^2+m^2$. The remaining terms give
$$
H=\frac12\int\frac{d^3p}{(2\pi)^3}\omega_{\mathbf p}
\left(a_{\mathbf p}^\dagger a_{\mathbf p}+a_{\mathbf p}a_{\mathbf p}^\dagger\right)
=\int\frac{d^3p}{(2\pi)^3}\omega_{\mathbf p}a_{\mathbf p}^\dagger a_{\mathbf p}
+\frac{(2\pi)^3\delta^3(0)}2\int\frac{d^3p}{(2\pi)^3}\omega_{\mathbf p}.
$$
This is a continuum of <quantum harmonic oscillators>. In a finite periodic volume $V$, $(2\pi)^3\delta^3(0)=V$. The <Fock vacuum> satisfies $a_{\mathbf p}|0\rangle=0$, so its <vacuum energy density> is
$$
\boxed{\mathcal E=\frac{\langle0|H|0\rangle}{V}=\frac12\int\frac{d^3p}{(2\pi)^3}\sqrt{\mathbf p^2+m^2}.}
$$
<Normal ordering> would subtract this constant; here it must be retained because it is the quantity being calculated.<Quantum Field Theory by David Tong>[Section 2, canonical quantization of the real scalar field and its zero-point Hamiltonian]{ref}

=== c
{scope}

==== Solution

A spherical three-<momentum> <ultraviolet cutoff> gives
$$
\mathcal E(\Lambda)=\frac1{4\pi^2}\int_0^\Lambda dp\,p^2\sqrt{p^2+m^2}
=\boxed{\frac{\Lambda\sqrt{\Lambda^2+m^2}(2\Lambda^2+m^2)-m^4\operatorname{arsinh}(\Lambda/m)}{32\pi^2}}.
$$
For $m>0$ and $\Lambda\gg m$, the <asymptotic expansion> is
$$
\boxed{\mathcal E(\Lambda)=\frac{\Lambda^4}{16\pi^2}+\frac{m^2\Lambda^2}{16\pi^2}
-\frac{m^4}{32\pi^2}\log\frac{2\Lambda}{m}+\frac{m^4}{128\pi^2}+O(m^6/\Lambda^2).}
$$
In particular, the leading <ultraviolet divergence> is quartic. For $m=0$, $\mathcal E=\Lambda^4/(16\pi^2)$ exactly.

Write $\varphi=\langle\psi\rangle$. Under the proposed identification, the <scalar potential from a field-dependent momentum cutoff> is $V_{\mathrm{ind}}(\varphi)=\mathcal E(|\varphi|)$. It is even and nonnegative, and its derivative with respect to $|\varphi|$ is $|\varphi|^2\sqrt{\varphi^2+m^2}/(4\pi^2)>0$ away from zero. Consequently its only <global minimum> is at $\varphi=0$: this contribution alone does not cause <spontaneous symmetry breaking>. At large $|\varphi|$ it grows quartically. At small $|\varphi|$ with fixed positive $m$, however,
$$
V_{\mathrm{ind}}(\varphi)=\frac{m|\varphi|^3}{12\pi^2}+O(|\varphi|^5/m).
$$
Thus the quartic sketch is an asymptotic shape, not the exact small-field behavior for a massive original <field>. Identifying a regulator with a new <field> is an additional modeling prescription; an actual <effective potential> also depends on the new <field>'s dynamics and any other allowed terms.

\Image[2018-cutoff-induced-potential.png]
{title=<Scalar potential> obtained by identifying a <ultraviolet cutoff> with a <scalar field> expectation value}

=== d
{scope}

==== Solution

Use one common <ultraviolet cutoff> for all physical degrees of freedom, with positive sign for each <bosonic> degree of freedom and negative sign for each <fermionic> degree of freedom. For the <mass-moment cancellation of vacuum energy divergences>, define the weighted <supertrace> $\operatorname{Str}f(m)=\sum_Bf(m_B)-\sum_Ff(m_F)$. The large-<momentum> expansion is
$$
\sum_B\sqrt{p^2+m_B^2}-\sum_F\sqrt{p^2+m_F^2}
=p\,\operatorname{Str}1+\frac{\operatorname{Str}m^2}{2p}-\frac{\operatorname{Str}m^4}{8p^3}+O(p^{-5}).
$$
After multiplication by the radial measure $p^2dp$, these three terms produce quartic, quadratic and logarithmic <ultraviolet divergences>. Therefore the necessary and sufficient cancellations for the displayed *unsubtracted* finite-species <vacuum energy> are
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{Str}1=0,\qquad\operatorname{Str}m^2=0,\qquad\operatorname{Str}m^4=0.}
$$
They respectively mean equal numbers of <bosonic> and <fermionic> physical degrees of freedom, equal sums of squared <masses>, and equal sums of fourth powers of <masses>. Once they hold, the remaining radial integrand falls as $p^{-3}$ and is integrable at infinity. Nonnegative <masses> introduce no lower-end divergence.

Pairing every <bosonic> degree of freedom with a <fermionic> one of the same <mass>, as in an unbroken <supersymmetric> spectrum, is sufficient and cancels the entire expression. It is stronger than these three moment conditions. For a concrete non-paired example, take bosonic squared <masses> $\{0,3,3\}m_*^2$ and fermionic squared <masses> $\{1,1,4\}m_*^2$. Both have three degrees of freedom, squared-<mass> sum $6m_*^2$ and fourth-power-<mass> sum $18m_*^4$, but their spectra differ. If a <vacuum energy> <counterterm> is instead permitted, a renormalized finite answer need not satisfy them; that is a different prescription from finiteness of the source's bare sum.

== 2
{scope}

=== a
{scope}

==== Solution

<Goldstone's theorem> states that a spontaneously broken continuous internal <global symmetry in field theory> of a local relativistic <quantum field theory> produces massless <Goldstone bosons>. For a <Lorentz invariance> <vacuum> breaking $G$ to its stabilizer $H$, there are $\dim G-\dim H$ independent symmetry-required massless directions. Additional accidentally massless modes are possible. A gauge redundancy does not obey this physical-state counting, and nonrelativistic systems need not have one independent mode per broken generator.

For the classical proof, consider real <scalar fields> $\phi_i$ with a positive canonical <kinetic term> and an invariant smooth <scalar potential> $V$. An infinitesimal generator $T^a$ satisfies
$$
\frac{\partial V}{\partial\phi_i}(T^a\phi)_i=0.
$$
Let $v$ be a stationary <vacuum> and differentiate this identity with respect to $\phi_j$. At $v$, the term proportional to $\partial_iV$ vanishes, leaving
$$
\boxed{\mathsf M^2_{ji}(T^av)_i=0,\qquad \mathsf M^2_{ji}=\left.\frac{\partial^2V}{\partial\phi_j\partial\phi_i}\right|_v.}
$$
The <Hessian matrix> $\mathsf M^2$ is the <scalar mass matrix>. An unbroken generator has $T^av=0$; broken generators span the tangent space of the <vacuum manifold> $G/H$. Each independent nonzero tangent vector is a zero <eigenvector> of $\mathsf M^2$. The quadratic <Euler-Lagrange equations> in these directions are $\Box\pi^a=0$, giving the massless <dispersion relation> $\omega^2=\mathbf p^2$. This proves the classical result and explains why the <Goldstone bosons> describe motion along the degenerate minima rather than against a restoring curvature.

=== b
{scope}

==== Solution

Both the canonical <kinetic term> and $V(\phi^{\mathsf T}\phi)$ are invariant under the <orthogonal group> $O(n)$. The stated nonzero <vacuum expectation value> has length $|v|$. Its stabilizer is the <orthogonal group> $O(n-1)$ acting on the perpendicular subspace. Hence
$$
\boxed{N_{\mathrm{GB}}=\frac{n(n-1)}2-\frac{(n-1)(n-2)}2=n-1.}
$$
The two nonzero coordinates of this particular <vacuum> do not break more generators than an axis-aligned <vacuum>: the two choices are related by a common <orthogonal transformation>.

Explicitly, with $s=\phi^{\mathsf T}\phi$,
$$
\mathsf M^2=2V'(v^2)\mathsf I+4V''(v^2)vv^{\mathsf T}.
$$
<stationary point> at nonzero $v$ requires $V'(v^2)=0$. The $n-1$ transverse <eigenvalues> vanish, while the radial squared <mass> is $4v^2V''(v^2)$. If this curvature also vanishes, there is an additional accidental massless radial mode; the number of symmetry-protected <Goldstone bosons> remains $n-1$. If $v=0$, the <symmetry> is unbroken and none is required.

=== c
{scope}

==== Solution

Assume $\mu^2,\lambda>0$. At zero source, the <vacuum manifold> is the sphere of radius $v_0=\mu/\sqrt\lambda$. The source tilts the <scalar potential> by $-\epsilon\phi_1$. Its force should be small compared with the radial restoring-force scale $\mu^2v_0$, so a useful quantitative condition is
$$
\boxed{|\epsilon|\ll\mu^2v_0=\frac{\mu^3}{\sqrt\lambda}.}
$$
For nonzero $\epsilon$, the exact <global symmetry in field theory> of the <Lagrangian> is $O(n-1)$, rotating $\phi_2,\ldots,\phi_n$. The global <vacuum> aligns with the source:
$$
\langle\phi\rangle=(\operatorname{sgn}(\epsilon)w,0,\ldots,0),\qquad
\lambda w^3-\mu^2w=|\epsilon|,\qquad
w=v_0+\frac{|\epsilon|}{2\mu^2}+O\!\left(\frac{\epsilon^2}{\mu^4v_0}\right).
$$
This <vacuum> preserves the entire remaining $O(n-1)$ <symmetry>. There is therefore no exact <Goldstone boson> for $\epsilon\ne0$. Expanding the <scalar potential> to quadratic order gives
$$
\boxed{m_{\perp}^2=-\mu^2+\lambda w^2=\frac{|\epsilon|}{w},\qquad
m_{\parallel}^2=-\mu^2+3\lambda w^2=2\mu^2+\frac{3|\epsilon|}{w}.}
$$
Thus there are $n-1$ degenerate light <pseudo-Goldstone bosons> and one heavy radial <scalar>. The small-source condition makes their squared <masses> parametrically smaller than the radial squared <mass>. Sending $\epsilon$ to zero restores the original massless modes.

=== d
{scope}

==== Solution

The breaking of the internal <global symmetry in field theory> $SU(n)\times SU(n)$ to $SU(n)$ removes $2(n^2-1)-(n^2-1)$ generators. The relativistic <Goldstone theorem> therefore requires
$$
\boxed{n^2-1\text{ massless Goldstone bosons}.}
$$
Other states fall into multiplets of the unbroken <special unitary group>, but their <masses> are not fixed by this counting. For the usual diagonal embedding $SU(n)_V\subset SU(n)_L\times SU(n)_R$, the <Goldstone bosons> form its adjoint multiplet.

If small explicit symmetry-breaking terms are added, the broken-generator directions generally acquire small curvature, producing light <pseudo-Goldstone bosons> rather than exactly massless states. Their squared <masses> tend to zero with those breaking parameters. Small breaking can also split multiplet degeneracies. These claims concern a small perturbation of the broken phase; approximate <symmetry> alone does not establish which <vacuum> is selected.

=== e
{scope}

==== Solution

The light states have the counting $3+4+1=8$. This suggests approximate <chiral symmetry breaking>
$$
\boxed{SU(3)_L\times SU(3)_R\longrightarrow SU(3)_V,\qquad 2(3^2-1)-(3^2-1)=8,}
$$
with a nonzero <quark chiral condensate> $\langle\bar q_R^{\,j}q_L^{\,i}\rangle\propto\delta^{ij}$ for the three light flavours $u,d,s$. The three <pions>, four <kaons> and predominantly octet <eta meson> are the resulting <pseudo-Goldstone bosons>. Their small nonzero <masses> reflect explicit <chiral symmetry> breaking by the light-quark <masses>; for example, $m_\pi^2$ is proportional to $m_u+m_d$ at leading order. The larger strange-quark <mass> makes the <kaons> and octet <eta meson> heavier than the <pions>, while <electromagnetic interactions> help split charged and neutral partners.<Gauge Theory by David Tong>[Chapter 5, chiral symmetry breaking and section 5.3 on adding quark masses]{ref}

Although the massless strong-interaction terms admit independent rotations of all six flavours, the charm, bottom and top <masses> are too large to treat six-flavour <chiral symmetry> as a small explicit breaking at the light-hadron scale. Full approximate $SU(6)_L\times SU(6)_R$ breaking would suggest 35 light modes, rather than this octet. The classical axial $U(1)$ is also broken by the <chiral anomaly>, so it does not supply a ninth equally light <Goldstone boson>.

**Terminology qualification:** these mesons have <spin> zero but negative <parity>: they are <pseudoscalar mesons>, with $J^P=0^-$, rather than positive-parity <scalar> <particles> with $J^P=0^+$. Calling the listed states “<scalar>” is acceptable only if it is being used loosely to mean <spin> zero.

== 3
{scope}

=== a
{scope}

==== Solution

The <action> is dimensionless and $[d^4x]=-4$, so $[\mathcal L]=4$. A canonical <scalar> <kinetic term> then requires $2+2[\phi]=4$, and the electromagnetic <kinetic term> requires $2[F_{\mu\nu}]=4$. Thus
$$
\boxed{[\Phi]=[a]=[b]=1,\qquad[F_{\mu\nu}]=2.}
$$
The brackets here denote <mass dimension>. The <Lagrangian> has no explicit coordinate dependence, and all indices are contracted as <Lorentz scalars>. Its continuous spacetime <symmetry> is the <Poincaré group>. <Noether's theorem> gives conserved <four-momentum> from translations and conserved <angular momentum>, including the boost generators, from <Lorentz transformations>. The nonzero <mass> $M$ and dimensionful <couplings> prevent a dilation <symmetry>.

**Sign qualification:** the printed electromagnetic <kinetic term> has the opposite healthy relative sign to the <scalar> <kinetic terms>. With the stated mostly-minus <Minkowski metric>, a physical <Maxwell Lagrangian> is $-F_{\mu\nu}F^{\mu\nu}/4$. The printed plus sign instead gives <photon> <Hamiltonian density> $-(\mathbf E^2+\mathbf B^2)/2$; a nonzero purely electric configuration already has negative <energy>, unbounded below as its amplitude grows. The physical <decay widths> below use the healthy Maxwell sign with the given interaction normalization. The dimensional and spacetime-symmetry conclusions themselves do not depend on this sign correction.

=== b
{scope}

==== i
{scope}

===== Solution

Counting the <mass dimensions> of each interaction operator gives
$$
\boxed{[\lambda_a]=-1,\qquad[\lambda_b]=-3,\qquad[\lambda_\gamma]=-1.}
$$
Indeed $\Phi(\partial a)^2$ and $\Phi F^2$ have dimension five, whereas $\Phi b^2(\partial b)^2$ has dimension seven. These are interactions of an <effective field theory> with dimensionful <couplings>.

The direct one-vertex <decay channels> are $\Phi\to aa$, $\Phi\to bbbb$ and $\Phi\to\gamma\gamma$. Each leading <scattering amplitude> is linear in its corresponding <coupling>, and a <decay width> is quadratic in the amplitude. Therefore, holding the parent <mass> fixed,
$$
\boxed{\Gamma_{aa}\propto|\lambda_a|^2,\qquad\Gamma_{bbbb}\propto|\lambda_b|^2,\qquad\Gamma_{\gamma\gamma}\propto|\lambda_\gamma|^2.}
$$
There are no tree-level interference terms between these different final states.

==== ii
{scope}

===== Solution

All daughter <particles> are massless, so the parent <mass> $M$ supplies the only kinematic scale at <tree-level Feynman diagram>[tree level]. Each vertex has two derivatives, making its <scattering amplitude> scale as $\lambda_iM^2$. For the four-$b$ vertex, symmetrizing over the derivative-bearing legs yields a sum of pairwise <four-momentum> products, which is proportional to $M^2$ by <four-momentum conservation>.

For $n$ massless daughters, the <Lorentz invariance> <Lorentz-invariant phase-space measure> scales as $M^{2n-4}$: each $d^3q/(2E)$ contributes two powers and the four-dimensional <Dirac delta> removes four powers. Including the parent factor $1/(2M)$ gives $\Gamma\sim|\lambda_i|^2M^{2n-1}$. Thus
$$
\boxed{\Gamma_{aa}\propto|\lambda_a|^2M^3,\qquad
\Gamma_{bbbb}\propto|\lambda_b|^2M^7,\qquad
\Gamma_{\gamma\gamma}\propto|\lambda_\gamma|^2M^3.}
$$
The unspecified factors are dimensionless angular integrals and identical-particle factors. Each result has <mass dimension> one, as a <decay width> must in <natural units>.

=== c
{scope}

==== Solution

Every interaction contains an even number of $b$ <fields>, so the exact discrete <symmetry> $b\mapsto-b$ forbids $\Phi\to bbb$ to every perturbative order. There is no such prohibition for $\Phi\to bb$.

The latter <decay channel> is absent at <tree-level Feynman diagram>[tree level] in the displayed interactions, but contracting two $b$ legs at a $\Phi b^2(\partial b)^2$ vertex produces a <tadpole diagram> with two external $b$ legs. For example, contracting the undifferentiated pair leaves the local operator $\Phi(\partial b)^2$ multiplied by an integral proportional to $\int d^4\ell/(\ell^2+i0)$. Contracting the differentiated pair instead gives a local $\Phi b^2$ contribution. Both are allowed <effective field theory> operators. With a <ultraviolet cutoff>, these local terms generically carry quadratic or quartic cutoff dependence and require matching coefficients or <counterterms>.

There is a relevant qualification to a claim that the one-loop <decay width> must be nonzero. Since $b$ is exactly massless here, the one-vertex <tadpole integrals> have no external scale and are <scaleless integrals>. They vanish in <dimensional regularization>. Thus the correct conclusion is **allowed, and generically generated by quantum corrections or matching**, not a regulator-independent nonzero one-loop prediction from $\lambda_b$ alone. No exact <symmetry> protects the two-$b$ channel; its numerical rate requires the renormalized coefficients of the allowed operators.

=== d
{scope}

==== Solution

Work in the parent <rest frame>, $p=(M,\mathbf0)$. For massless daughters, the spatial <Dirac delta> fixes $\mathbf q_2=-\mathbf q_1$ and $E_1=E_2=q$. The <Lorentz invariance> two-body <Lorentz-invariant phase-space measure> becomes
$$
\begin{aligned}
d\Pi_2&=\frac{(2\pi)^4}{(2\pi)^6}\frac{q^2dq\,d\Omega}{4q^2}\,\delta(M-2q)\\
&=\frac{dq\,d\Omega}{16\pi^2}\,\delta(M-2q)
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
\boxed{d\Pi_2=\frac{d\Omega}{32\pi^2}.}
\end{aligned}
$$
The factor $1/2$ in the last step is the Jacobian of the energy-conservation <Dirac delta>. Consequently the labelled two-body <decay width> is
$$
\boxed{\Gamma_{\mathrm{labelled}}=\frac1{64\pi^2M}\int d\Omega\,|\mathcal A|^2.}
$$
For two identical final <particles>, the full angular integral counts each unordered final state twice, so the physical <decay width> includes an additional factor $1/2!$. This factor is needed for both channels in part e; it is separate from any vertex combinatorics already included in the <Feynman rule>.

=== e
{scope}

==== Solution

The massless two-body <kinematics> gives $q_1\cdot q_2=M^2/2$. The <scalar> <scattering amplitude> therefore has constant magnitude $|\mathcal A_{aa}|=|\lambda_a|M^2/2$. Use $\int d\Omega=4\pi$ and the identical-particle factor from part d:
$$
\boxed{\Gamma_{aa}=\frac1{2!}\frac{4\pi}{64\pi^2M}\frac{|\lambda_a|^2M^4}{4}
=\frac{|\lambda_a|^2M^3}{128\pi}.}
$$
For the <photons>, the <two-photon vertex of a scalar field-strength interaction> following from $\Phi F_{\mu\nu}F^{\mu\nu}$ is, up to an irrelevant overall phase,
$$
\mathcal A_{\gamma\gamma}=\lambda_\gamma\left[(q_1\cdot q_2)(\epsilon_1\cdot\epsilon_2)
-(q_1\cdot\epsilon_2)(q_2\cdot\epsilon_1)\right].
$$
**Vertex qualification:** the source prints same-index <polarization> contractions in the second product, whereas the contractions must be crossed as above. The printed expression fails the <Ward identity>. For a concrete counterexample take $q_1=(E,0,0,E)$, $q_2=(E,0,0,-E)$ and $\epsilon_2=(1,1,0,-1)$. This is a transverse unit spacelike <photon polarization vector>, since $q_2\cdot\epsilon_2=0$ and $\epsilon_2^2=-1$. Replacing $\epsilon_1$ by $q_1$ in the printed expression leaves $4\lambda_\gamma E^3$ instead of zero. The corrected crossed expression vanishes under either such replacement.

Choose physical spatial transverse <photon polarizations> along $x$ and $y$ in the parent <rest frame>. Then both crossed products vanish; the two equal-polarization combinations have $|\epsilon_1\cdot\epsilon_2|^2=1$ and the two orthogonal combinations give zero. There is no initial-spin average for a spin-zero parent. Hence
$$
\sum_{\mathrm{pol}}|\mathcal A_{\gamma\gamma}|^2=2|\lambda_\gamma|^2\left(\frac{M^2}{2}\right)^2
=\frac{|\lambda_\gamma|^2M^4}{2},
\qquad
\boxed{\Gamma_{\gamma\gamma}=\frac{|\lambda_\gamma|^2M^3}{64\pi}.}
$$
The calculation in this spatial transverse gauge also yields the intended result from the misprinted expression, but only the corrected vertex makes its <gauge invariance> explicit. For equal-magnitude <couplings>, the <photon> <decay width> is twice the two-scalar <decay width> because of its two nonzero <polarization> combinations.

== 4
{scope}

=== a
{scope}

==== Solution

Let $\beta=1/(k_BT)$ and use the two <spin> values $+1,-1$ as row and column labels. Define the <spin-chain transfer matrix>
$$
W_{st}=e^{-\beta E_0(s,t)},\qquad s,t\in\{+1,-1\}.
$$
It is a real <symmetric matrix> because $E_0(s,t)=E_0(t,s)$; every entry is strictly positive for finite bond <energies> at $T>0$. The <Boltzmann factor> of a <spin> configuration factorizes into the product $W_{\sigma_1\sigma_2}\cdots W_{\sigma_L\sigma_1}$. Summing the intermediate <spin> labels is exactly <matrix multiplication>, and the final closure imposes <periodic boundary conditions>. Thus
$$
\boxed{Z_L=\sum_{\sigma_1,\ldots,\sigma_L}\prod_{j=1}^LW_{\sigma_j\sigma_{j+1}}=\operatorname{Tr}W^L.}
$$
Writing $W=\left(\begin{smallmatrix}a&b\\b&d\end{smallmatrix}\right)$, its <eigenvalues> are
$$
\lambda_\pm=\frac{a+d\pm\sqrt{(a-d)^2+4b^2}}2,
\qquad \boxed{Z_L=\lambda_+^L+\lambda_-^L.}
$$
A positive-entry <matrix> need not have two positive <eigenvalues>; $\lambda_-$ can be negative. The <Perron–Frobenius theorem> ensures $\lambda_+>|\lambda_-|$ here. Therefore the <Helmholtz free energy> is $F_L=-k_BT\ln Z_L$, and its value per <spin> in the <thermodynamic limit> is $-k_BT\ln\lambda_+$.

=== b
{scope}

==== Solution

For an open chain there are $L-1$ bonds, with both endpoint <Ising spins> independently summed. Retain the same <spin-chain transfer matrix>, but replace the trace by endpoint vectors. With $\mathbf1=(1,1)^{\mathsf T}$,
$$
\boxed{Z_L^{\mathrm{open}}=\mathbf1^{\mathsf T}W^{L-1}\mathbf1.}
$$
This follows directly by expanding the product of $L-1$ <Boltzmann factors>. If $\mathbf v_\pm$ are orthonormal <eigenvectors> of the <symmetric matrix> $W$, the equivalent <spectral decomposition> is
$$
Z_L^{\mathrm{open}}=\sum_{\nu=\pm}(\mathbf1^{\mathsf T}\mathbf v_\nu)^2\lambda_\nu^{L-1}.
$$
Fixed endpoint <Ising spins> replace the corresponding $\mathbf1$ by a basis vector; extra endpoint <energies> replace its entries by the corresponding <Boltzmann factors>. The leading bulk <Helmholtz free energy> per <spin> agrees with the periodic result, because the boundary changes only subextensive terms. In particular, $L=1$ gives $Z_1^{\mathrm{open}}=\mathbf1^{\mathsf T}\mathbf1=2$, as required for one free <Ising spin>.

=== c
{scope}

==== Solution

Let $M=L/2$, so that the total number of <Ising spins> is $3M$. Write $K=\beta J$ and group each triangle's two base spins and its apex into one cell. The <Ising chain with alternating triangular decorations> has a triangle bond followed by an undecorated connecting bond. Summing an apex <Ising spin> first gives a decorated-bond <Boltzmann weight>
$$
A_{st}=\sum_{\tau=\pm1}e^{-K[st+(s+t)\tau]}=2e^{-Kst}\cosh[K(s+t)].
$$
The undecorated bridge has <Boltzmann weight> $B_{ts'}=e^{-Kts'}$. In the basis $+1,-1$,
$$
A=\begin{pmatrix}e^K+e^{-3K}&2e^K\\2e^K&e^K+e^{-3K}\end{pmatrix},\qquad
B=\begin{pmatrix}e^{-K}&e^K\\e^K&e^{-K}\end{pmatrix}.
$$
Each cell contributes $T=AB$, so <periodic boundary conditions> give
$$
\boxed{Z_L=\operatorname{Tr}(AB)^M.}
$$
The two <matrices> have common normalized <eigenvectors> $(1,1)^{\mathsf T}/\sqrt2$ and $(1,-1)^{\mathsf T}/\sqrt2$, expressing <spin inversion symmetry>. Their respective <eigenvalues> are
$$
a_+=3e^K+e^{-3K},\quad a_-=e^{-3K}-e^K,\qquad
b_+=e^{-K}+e^K,\quad b_-=e^{-K}-e^K.
$$
Consequently the cell <transfer matrix> has <eigenvalues>
$$
\begin{aligned}
\lambda_+&=(3e^K+e^{-3K})(e^{-K}+e^K)=3+e^{-4K}+e^{-2K}+3e^{2K},\\
\lambda_-&=(e^{-3K}-e^K)(e^{-K}-e^K)=e^{-4K}-e^{-2K}-1+e^{2K},
\end{aligned}
$$
and the finite-size <partition function> is
$$
\boxed{Z_L=\lambda_+^{L/2}+\lambda_-^{L/2}.}
$$
For $K>0$, $\lambda_-\geq0$ and $\lambda_+-\lambda_-=4+2e^{-2K}+2e^{2K}>0$, so only $\lambda_+$ survives in the <thermodynamic limit>. Divide the <Helmholtz free energy> by $3M$, the number of sites rather than the number of cells or base spins:
$$
\boxed{f(T)=-\frac{k_BT}{3}\ln\lambda_+
=-\frac{k_BT}{3}\ln\!\left(3+e^{-4\beta J}+e^{-2\beta J}+3e^{2\beta J}\right).}
$$
As a counting check, $K=0$ gives $\lambda_+=8$, $\lambda_-=0$, hence $Z_L=8^M=2^{3M}$, exactly the number of independent <Ising spin> configurations. This model has separated decorated bonds and should not be confused with the corner-sharing <sawtooth Ising chain>, which has a different site count.

=== d
{scope}

==== Solution

For low <temperature>, factor out the largest <Boltzmann factor> from the dominant <eigenvalue>:
$$
\lambda_+=3e^{2K}\left(1+e^{-2K}+\frac13e^{-4K}+\frac13e^{-6K}\right).
$$
Thus the <Helmholtz free energy> per site has the expansion
$$
f=-\frac{2J}{3}-\frac{k_BT}{3}\ln3-\frac{k_BT}{3}e^{-2K}+O\!\left(k_BT e^{-4K}\right).
$$
Differentiate at fixed $J$, remembering that $K=J/(k_BT)$. The <entropy> per site is
$$
s=\frac{k_B}{3}\ln3+\frac{k_B}{3}(1+2K)e^{-2K}+O\!\left(k_B(1+K)e^{-4K}\right),
\qquad \boxed{\lim_{T\to0^+}s(T)=\frac{k_B}{3}\ln3.}
$$
The nonzero <residual entropy> comes from an exponentially large <ground-state degeneracy>, generated by <geometrical frustration>. A triangle with antiferromagnetic <energy> $J[st+(s+t)\tau]$ has minimum <energy> $-J$: if $s=t$, the apex must have the opposite sign; if $s=-t$, either apex sign is permitted. The two all-equal configurations instead have <energy> $3J$. Thus exactly one of a minimum-energy triangle's bonds is frustrated. The bridge can simultaneously minimize its <energy> at $-J$ by requiring $t=-s'$, where $s'$ is the next cell's first base <spin>.

For fixed $s$ and $s'$, the number of allowed apex choices is therefore two when $s=s'$ and one when $s=-s'$. The zero-temperature counting <transfer matrix> on successive first base spins is
$$
G=\begin{pmatrix}2&1\\1&2\end{pmatrix},\qquad
\boxed{g_M=\operatorname{Tr}G^M=3^M+1,\qquad E_{\mathrm{GS}}=-2JM=-JL.}
$$
The <eigenvalues> of $G$ are $3$ and $1$. Dividing $k_B\ln g_M$ by the $3M$ sites and taking the <thermodynamic limit> gives $k_B\ln3/3$, independently confirming the derivative calculation. This does not assert a unique ordered <ground state>: the extensive choice of frustrated bonds is precisely what leaves <entropy> at zero <temperature>. At high <temperature>, all <Ising spins> become independent and $s\to k_B\ln2$ per site.

== 5
{scope}

=== a
{scope}

==== Solution

With no pairing, the <Hamiltonian> is diagonal in the <fermionic occupation numbers>. The <Pauli exclusion principle> allows each <momentum> mode to be either empty or occupied once. Without a fixed <fermion number> constraint, occupying a mode lowers the <energy> precisely when its single-particle <energy> is negative. Therefore, with $|0\rangle$ the <Fock vacuum>, a <ground state> is
$$
\boxed{|\mathrm{GS}\rangle=\prod_{p:\,\epsilon(p)<0}c_p^\dagger|0\rangle,\qquad E_{\mathrm{GS}}=\sum_{p:\,\epsilon(p)<0}\epsilon(p).}
$$
Choose a fixed ordering of the <fermionic creation operators>; changing it alters only the overall <phase>. Any zero-energy modes may be occupied or empty without changing the <ground-state energy>.

To identify the occupied modes, complete the square:
$$
\frac{\epsilon(p)}\alpha=\left(p^2-\frac\beta2\right)^2+\mu-\frac{\beta^2}{4}.
$$
Here $\beta$ is the dispersion parameter, not <inverse temperature>. If $\beta^2<4\mu$, every mode has positive <energy> and the unique <ground state> is the <Fock vacuum>, with $E_{\mathrm{GS}}=0$. If $\beta^2=4\mu$, there are still no negative-energy modes, but $p=\pm\sqrt{\beta/2}$ have zero <energy> if they lie on the allowed <momentum> grid. Each such mode doubles the <ground-state degeneracy>.

For $\beta^2>4\mu$, define
$$
p_-^2=\frac{\beta-\sqrt{\beta^2-4\mu}}2,\qquad
p_+^2=\frac{\beta+\sqrt{\beta^2-4\mu}}2.
$$
Both are positive, and the occupied <Fermi sea> consists of the two intervals $p_-<|p|<p_+$. With $p=2\pi n/L$, the requested finite-size <ground-state energy> is explicitly
$$
\boxed{E_{\mathrm{GS}}=2\alpha\!\sum_{\substack{n\in\mathbb Z_{>0}\\ Lp_-/(2\pi)<n<Lp_+/(2\pi)}}\left[\left(\frac{2\pi n}L\right)^4-\beta\left(\frac{2\pi n}L\right)^2+\mu\right].}
$$
The factor two counts opposite <momenta>. Endpoints are excluded from this <sum> because they have zero <energy>; when an endpoint is allowed, its optional occupation adds <ground-state degeneracy>. A narrow negative-energy interval can contain no allowed finite-size <momenta>, in which case the <sum> is empty. The zero-momentum mode is always empty since $\epsilon(0)=\alpha\mu>0$.

=== b
{scope}

==== Solution

For real angles write $u_p=\cos\theta_p$ and $v_p=\sin\theta_p$. For one independent pair $p>0$, the transformation and its conjugate read
$$
c_p=u_p\alpha_p+i v_p\alpha_{-p}^\dagger,\qquad
c_{-p}=u_p\alpha_{-p}-i v_p\alpha_p^\dagger.
$$
Using the <canonical anticommutation relations> for the $\alpha$ modes gives
$$
\begin{aligned}
\{c_p,c_p^\dagger\}&=u_p^2+v_p^2=1,\\
\{c_{-p},c_{-p}^\dagger\}&=u_p^2+v_p^2=1,\\
\{c_p,c_{-p}\}&=-i u_pv_p+i v_pu_p=0,\\
\{c_p,c_{-p}^\dagger\}&=0.
\end{aligned}
$$
The remaining <anticommutators> vanish for independent <momentum> pairs. The displayed two-by-two <matrix> is <unitary> and its inverse is obtained by changing $i$ to $-i$.

When the same formula is indexed over both signs of <momentum>, its first row at $-p$ must agree with the conjugate of its second row at $p$. Thus a consistent global convention requires
$$
\boxed{u_{-p}=u_p,\qquad v_{-p}=-v_p,\qquad \theta_{-p}=-\theta_p\pmod{2\pi}.}
$$
This is the <odd-momentum fermionic Bogoliubov angle> convention. If one checks only the first-row formulas, their cross <anticommutator> is $i\sin(\theta_p+\theta_{-p})$; its vanishing gives the condition modulo $\pi$, with possible signs absorbable in the $\alpha$ modes. Requiring both rows to describe the same operators fixes the convention above. Merely checking a separate <unitary matrix> at each <momentum> misses this redundancy: choosing $\theta_p=\theta_{-p}=\pi/4$ in both first rows would give $\{c_p,c_{-p}\}=i$.

At $p=0$, the <annihilation operator> and its conjugate belong to one mode. The full displayed formula is consistent only with $v_0=0$; choose $\theta_0=0$ and $\alpha_0=c_0$. This mode is not a two-mode pair.

=== c
{scope}

==== Solution

Group the nonzero modes into independent pairs $p>0$. For one pair set $\varepsilon=\epsilon(p)=p^2/(2m)+\mu$, and introduce the <Nambu spinor> $\Psi_p=(c_p,c_{-p}^\dagger)^{\mathsf T}$. <Normal ordering> using $c_{-p}c_{-p}^\dagger=1-c_{-p}^\dagger c_{-p}$ gives
$$
H_p=\Psi_p^\dagger\begin{pmatrix}\varepsilon&i\Delta\\-i\Delta&-\varepsilon\end{pmatrix}\Psi_p+\varepsilon.
$$
This exactly contains both <kinetic terms> and the pairing terms once; there is no additional factor of one half because only $p>0$ is summed.

Insert $\Psi_p=U_p\mathcal A_p$, where $\mathcal A_p=(\alpha_p,\alpha_{-p}^\dagger)^{\mathsf T}$ and $U_p$ is the <Bogoliubov transformation> <matrix>. Direct <matrix multiplication> yields
$$
U_p^\dagger h_pU_p=\begin{pmatrix}
\varepsilon\cos2\theta_p-\Delta\sin2\theta_p&i(\varepsilon\sin2\theta_p+\Delta\cos2\theta_p)\\
-i(\varepsilon\sin2\theta_p+\Delta\cos2\theta_p)&-\varepsilon\cos2\theta_p+\Delta\sin2\theta_p
\end{pmatrix}.
$$
Choose the diagonalizing branch
$$
\boxed{\cos2\theta_p=\frac{\varepsilon}{E_p},\qquad \sin2\theta_p=-\frac\Delta{E_p},\qquad E_p=\sqrt{\varepsilon^2+\Delta^2},\quad p>0.}
$$
Then the off-diagonal terms vanish and the upper diagonal entry is $E_p>0$. Extend the angle to negative <momenta> as in part b; the sine relation just written applies to the positive representative of each pair. The <BCS coherence factors> can be chosen as
$$
u_p=\sqrt{\frac{E_p+\varepsilon}{2E_p}},\qquad
v_p=-\frac{\Delta}{\sqrt{2E_p(E_p+\varepsilon)}}.
$$
Here $u_p$ denotes $\cos\theta_p$, and $v_p$ denotes $\sin\theta_p$; these expressions are nonsingular because $\mu>0$ and $m>0$. The minus sign of $v_p$ for positive $\Delta$ is fixed by the pairing <phase> in this <Hamiltonian>.

A second use of the <canonical anticommutation relations> gives
$$
H_p=E_p\left(\alpha_p^\dagger\alpha_p-\alpha_{-p}\alpha_{-p}^\dagger\right)+\varepsilon
=E_p\left(\alpha_p^\dagger\alpha_p+\alpha_{-p}^\dagger\alpha_{-p}\right)+\varepsilon-E_p.
$$
The zero-momentum term was never paired and is simply $\mu\alpha_0^\dagger\alpha_0$. Hence
$$
\boxed{H=\sum_pE(p)\alpha_p^\dagger\alpha_p+C,\qquad
C=\sum_{p>0}\bigl[\epsilon(p)-\sqrt{\epsilon(p)^2+\Delta^2}\bigr],}
$$
with
$$
\boxed{E(0)=\mu,\qquad E(p)=\sqrt{\epsilon(p)^2+\Delta^2}\quad(p\ne0).}
$$
The constant is the <ground-state energy>. At fixed $L$, its ultraviolet tail converges: $\epsilon(p)-E(p)=-\Delta^2/[\epsilon(p)+E(p)]\sim-m\Delta^2/p^2$ in one dimension. Replacing $E(0)$ by $\sqrt{\mu^2+\Delta^2}$ would incorrectly pair a spinless mode with itself.

=== d
{scope}

==== Solution

Since every <quasiparticle> <energy> is positive, the <ground state> is the <vacuum> of all the $\alpha_p$ <annihilation operators>, including $\alpha_0=c_0$. Its zero-momentum mode is therefore empty. For each positive <momentum>, the inverse <Bogoliubov transformation> gives
$$
\alpha_p=u_pc_p-i v_pc_{-p}^\dagger,\qquad
\alpha_{-p}=u_pc_{-p}+i v_pc_p^\dagger.
$$
Both annihilate the normalized pair state $(u_p+i v_pc_p^\dagger c_{-p}^\dagger)|0\rangle$. For example, applying $\alpha_p$ produces the two cancelling terms $i u_pv_pc_{-p}^\dagger|0\rangle$. Thus
$$
\boxed{|\Omega\rangle=\prod_{p>0}\left(u_p+i v_pc_p^\dagger c_{-p}^\dagger\right)|0\rangle,\qquad c_0|\Omega\rangle=0.}
$$
The factors have even <fermion parity> and commute for disjoint <momentum> pairs. Each normalized factor is a <superposition> of zero and two <fermions>, whose pair-occupation <probability> is $v_p^2$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\langle\Omega|\widehat N|\Omega\rangle=2\sum_{p>0}v_p^2=\sum_{p>0}\left(1-\frac{\epsilon(p)}{E_p}\right).}
$$
There is no zero-mode contribution. For fixed $L$, the <sum> converges since $v_p^2=O(p^{-4})$.

A normalized simple excited state is $|k\rangle=\alpha_k^\dagger|\Omega\rangle$ for $k>0$. Its <excitation energy> is $E_k$, and its total <momentum> is $k$: both terms in $\alpha_k^\dagger=u_kc_k^\dagger+i v_kc_{-k}$ add <momentum> $k$. Acting on the $k,-k$ pair gives
$$
\alpha_k^\dagger\left(u_k+i v_kc_k^\dagger c_{-k}^\dagger\right)|0\rangle
=(u_k^2+v_k^2)c_k^\dagger|0\rangle=c_k^\dagger|0\rangle.
$$
The affected pair now contains exactly one <fermion> instead of mean number $2v_k^2$; all other pairs are unchanged. Consequently
$$
\boxed{\langle k|\widehat N|k\rangle=\langle\widehat N\rangle_\Omega+1-2v_k^2
=\langle\widehat N\rangle_\Omega+\frac{\epsilon(k)}{E_k}.}
$$
This is the <fermion number of a Bogoliubov excitation>. Its change in mean <fermion number> lies between zero and one for $\mu>0$, with the value one at $\Delta=0$. The <Bogoliubov quasiparticle> coherently mixes adding a <fermion> and removing the opposite-momentum <fermion>; it is not an eigen-excitation of the original <fermion number operator>. Indeed,
$$
[\widehat N,H]=2i\Delta\sum_{p>0}\left(c_p^\dagger c_{-p}^\dagger+c_{-p}c_p\right),
$$
so <fermion number> is not conserved when $\Delta\ne0$, while <fermion parity> is conserved. The generally noninteger change is an <expectation value> in a coherent paired background, rather than a fractional <eigenvalue> of the <fermion number operator>. In the weak-pairing limit at fixed $k$, it tends to one, as expected for an ordinary added <fermion>.

== 6
{scope}

=== a
{scope}

==== Solution

Choose a coarse-grained <magnetization> $m(\mathbf x)$ as the <order parameter>: it vanishes in the <paramagnetism> phase and develops a spontaneous nonzero value in the <ferromagnetism> phase. In zero applied <magnetic field>, <spin> reversal sends $m\mapsto-m$, so the local <Landau free energy> contains only even powers. A minimal spatially inhomogeneous <free-energy functional> is
$$
\mathcal F[m]=F_{\mathrm{reg}}(T)+\int d^dx\left[\frac{a(T)}2m^2+\frac b4m^4+\frac c6m^6
+\frac\kappa2|\nabla m|^2-hm+\cdots\right],\qquad\kappa>0.
$$
The positive <gradient> coefficient penalizes rapid spatial variations. Near an ordinary transition take $a(T)=a_0(T-T_0)$, $a_0>0$, and positive $b$; retain the sextic term when $b$ can approach or cross zero. The source $h$ is conjugate to the <magnetization> and explicitly breaks <spin> reversal.

Determine equilibrium by minimizing this <free-energy functional>, imposing the appropriate <boundary conditions>. Its <Euler-Lagrange equation> is
$$
\boxed{-\kappa\nabla^2m+a(T)m+bm^3+cm^5-h=0.}
$$
For a uniform system at $h=0$, compare the values of all stable stationary solutions, rather than accepting every solution of this equation. The selected <global minimum> gives the equilibrium <magnetization>; derivatives of its <free energy> give <entropy>, <heat capacity> and <magnetic susceptibility>. At an ordinary transition the two ordered minima emerge continuously from zero. Spatial dependence also permits <domain walls> and an estimate of the <correlation length>.

This is a <mean-field approximation>: it selects a minimizing profile and neglects statistical fluctuations around it. The <symmetry> constrains the expansion, but its numerical coefficients are phenomenological. Close enough to a transition, <thermal fluctuations> near a <critical point> can invalidate the resulting <mean-field critical exponents>.<Statistical Field Theory by David Tong>[Chapter 1, the Landau-Ginzburg free-energy functional, spatial gradients and mean-field phase transitions]{ref}

=== b
{scope}

==== Solution

For the sextic polynomial to be bounded below at large $|m|$ while allowing either sign of $b$, its leading coefficient must obey
$$
\boxed{c>0.}
$$
If $c<0$, the <free energy> tends to $-\infty$ as $|m|\to\infty$, so it cannot define an equilibrium within this truncation. The exceptional case $c=0$ is stable for $b>0$ and gives the ordinary quartic <Landau theory>; it cannot stabilize the negative-$b$ region required for the full sextic <phase diagram>. Higher positive powers could stabilize a negative $c$, but they are absent from this model.

=== c
{scope}

==== Solution

Fix $c>0$. <Stationary points> satisfy $m(a+bm^2+cm^4)=0$. With $x=m^2$, the nonzero candidates are
$$
x_\pm=\frac{-b\pm\sqrt{b^2-4ac}}{2c}.
$$
Keep only real positive roots. At such a point,
$$
F''(m)=2x(b+2cx),
$$
so $x_+$ gives <local minima> and $x_-$ gives local maxima when both exist. The solution $m=0$ is a <local minimum> for $a>0$. To choose the phase, compare <free energies>: using the <stationary point> relation $a=-bx-cx^2$ gives
$$
F(m)-F(0)=-\frac b4x^2-\frac c3x^3.
$$
For $b>0$, this expression is negative whenever a nonzero stationary minimum exists. The <paramagnetism> phase is stable for $a>0$, and the <ferromagnetism> phase with $m=\pm\sqrt{x_+}$ is stable for $a<0$. The <phase transition> at $a=0$, $b>0$, is continuous and second order.

For $b<0$, equality with $F(0)$ requires $x=-3b/(4c)$. Substitution into the stationary equation gives the first-order coexistence curve
$$
\boxed{a_{\mathrm t}=\frac{3b^2}{16c},\quad b<0,\qquad
m_{\mathrm t}^2=-\frac{3b}{4c}.}
$$
The disordered phase is the <global minimum> above this curve, and the ordered phase is the <global minimum> below it. At coexistence the factorization
$$
\boxed{F(m)=\frac c6m^2\left(m^2+\frac{3b}{4c}\right)^2}
$$
exhibits three equally low minima, $m=0$ and $m=\pm\sqrt{-3b/(4c)}$, proving the discontinuous jump.

The ordered <spinodal> is $a=b^2/(4c)$, $b<0$, where its <local minimum> meets the intervening local maximum. The disordered <spinodal> is $a=0$, $b<0$. Thus the ordered phase is metastable for $a_{\mathrm t}<a<b^2/(4c)$; the disordered phase is metastable for $0<a<a_{\mathrm t}$. These <spinodals> are limits of <metastability>, not equilibrium transition curves.

The first-order curve meets the continuous transition line at the <tricritical point> $(a,b)=(0,0)$. On the specially tuned path $b=0$, the equilibrium ordered solution is $m=\pm(-a/c)^{1/4}$ for $a<0$ and tends continuously to zero at the origin.

\Image[2018-sextic-landau-phase-diagram.png]
{title=<Phase diagram> of the sextic <Landau free energy>, including coexistence, <spinodals> and the <tricritical point>}

=== d
{scope}

==== Solution

Write $a=a_0(T-T_c)$, $a_0>0$, at fixed $b\geq0$, and define the <critical exponent> by $|m|\sim(T_c-T)^\beta$ on the ordered side. For fixed $b>0$ and small negative $a$, the stationary equation gives $m^2=-a/b+O(a^2)$. Consequently
$$
\boxed{\beta=\frac12\quad\text{on the ordinary line }a=0,\ b>0.}
$$
At the <tricritical point> approached along the tuned path $b=0$, the stationary equation instead gives $m^4=-a/c$, so
$$
\boxed{\beta_{\mathrm{tri}}=\frac14\quad\text{at }a=b=0\text{ along }b=0.}
$$
For fixed $b<0$, the transition occurs before $a$ reaches zero and the <magnetization> jumps; it is not a second-order transition and has no vanishing-order-parameter exponent of this form. A path that does not keep the quartic coefficient tuned to zero generally crosses over to the ordinary result.

=== e
{scope}

==== Solution

Take $a=a_0(T-T_c)$ and hold $b,c$ fixed. The <heat capacity> comes from the minimized <free energy>, $C=-T\,d^2F_{\min}/dT^2$, with any smooth background added separately. For $b>0$ and $T<T_c$,
$$
F_{\min}-F_{\mathrm{reg}}=-\frac{a^2}{4b}+O(a^3),\qquad
C_{\mathrm{sing},-}=\frac{T a_0^2}{2b}+O(T_c-T).
$$
Above $T_c$, $m=0$ and the polynomial contributes zero. There is a finite jump, rather than a divergence: the conventional ordinary <mean-field critical exponents> are
$$
\boxed{\alpha_-=\alpha_+=0\quad(b>0),\qquad \Delta C=\frac{T_ca_0^2}{2b}.}
$$
The upper branch has zero order-parameter contribution; a finite regular <heat capacity> has exponent zero.

Along the tuned tricritical path $b=0$, the ordered minimum gives
$$
F_{\min}-F_{\mathrm{reg}}=-\frac{(-a)^{3/2}}{3\sqrt c},\qquad
\boxed{C_{\mathrm{sing},-}=\frac{T a_0^2}{4\sqrt c\sqrt{-a}}
=\frac{T a_0^{3/2}}{4\sqrt c}(T_c-T)^{-1/2},\quad \alpha_-^{\mathrm{tri}}=\frac12.}
$$
**Upper-side qualification:** the <vanishing disordered-side heat-capacity amplitude in Landau theory> matters here: for $b=0$ and $a>0$, the minimizing solution is still exactly $m=0$, so this saddle-point polynomial gives $F_{\min}-F_{\mathrm{reg}}=0$ and $C_{\mathrm{sing},+}=0$. A zero function cannot determine a nonzero-amplitude power-law exponent. For example, adding the allowed smooth background $F_{\mathrm{reg}}=-C_0T\log(T/T_*)$, with $C_0>0$, leaves the entire <phase diagram> unchanged but makes the upper-side <heat capacity> exactly $C_0$, not a $(T-T_c)^{-1/2}$ divergence.

One convention assigns the tricritical scaling exponent $\alpha=1/2$ to both sides, writing $C_{\mathrm{sing},\pm}=A_\pm|T-T_c|^{-1/2}$ with $A_+=0$ at this <mean-field approximation> saddle. If the source's literal $C\sim|T-T_c|^{-\alpha_+}$ is meant to have a nonzero leading coefficient, the given polynomial establishes **no upper-side tricritical singular exponent**; with a nonzero smooth background its leading upper-side exponent is zero. A nonzero upper-side singularity requires fluctuation information beyond this minimization. This distinction prevents claiming an upper-side divergence that the displayed <Landau free energy> does not produce.
